{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -130 collab/exception-track/1794402000000-architect-py-r2r3-step7-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log; rg -n 'retained|Weak|token' src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc; git branch --show-current\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1caba-a78a-7e33-97f7-e1218ee971d2\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the architecture and ruling, then trace retention, reception, and egress in the target worktree. I’ll keep the review read-only and report findings with a verdict.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g 'SKILL.md' -g 'datom.world.md' -g '*ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g 'SKILL.md' -g 'datom.world.md' -g '*ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld\\ncollab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md\\nAGENTS.md\\nr3-fix-wt/AGENTS.md\\nr3-fix-wt/docs/design/datom.world.md\\nr3-fix-wt/collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: code-review-pr\\ndescription: >-\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\n---\\n\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\n\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\n\\n# 2. Review last commit\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\n\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\n\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Code Review Workflow\\n\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\n\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\n\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\n\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\n\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\n\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\n  ```diff\\n  - problematic code\\n  + suggested fix\\n  ```\\n\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\n\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\n\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Utility Script Reference\\n\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\n\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\n\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -8 --oneline && git diff 4cfa609d..a396ae28 --stat && cat AGENTS.md && cat collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && python3 -B /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target 4cfa609d..a396ae28 --repo-dir /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’m using the `code-review-pr` skill for the review structure. The checkout is at `16c6294f`; I’ll distinguish the three runtime commits from the later test and diagnosis commits.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -8 --oneline && git diff 4cfa609d..a396ae28 --stat && cat AGENTS.md && cat collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-SyMyyGUv' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:12.408 xcodebuild[61339:122688134]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:12.872 xcodebuild[61339:122688132] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-3Ijy2aX0' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:13.933 xcodebuild[61350:122688240]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:14.160 xcodebuild[61350:122688239] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-yOGnNztH' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:16.217 xcodebuild[61364:122688381]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:16.481 xcodebuild[61364:122688377] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-PjA42kMr' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:17.440 xcodebuild[61384:122688435]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:17.702 xcodebuild[61384:122688429] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n16c6294f docs(exception): trace python acceptance red to baseline overlay\\nbe87df74 test(python): pin injected-condition and acceptance batch paths\\n04cb7887 test(yin.vm.semantic): regenerate the :c4 :py scanner pins after the py-r2r3 prelude repairs — the deferred set is this run's single mover (:py); three-run byte-identical; the untouched groups verified in place by the abort-gate\\na396ae28 python egress: forward a retained generated exception's original condition\\n24f2f467 python R3 reception: retain the complete protocol condition\\nd9a73150 python R2 reception: wrap foreign payloads in a generated ForeignException\\n4cfa609d test(yin.vm.macro-build): read :kind from the post-U1 event-row slot\\n8cd9fc92 merge: the B2 arc collab artifacts (r2-r4 findings, argon b2r4 confirm) from feat/p5b2-lifecycle\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-zB8YSPNq' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:20.744 xcodebuild[61402:122688639]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:21.169 xcodebuild[61402:122688636] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-SCjMum4Y' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:22.588 xcodebuild[61418:122688823]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:22.886 xcodebuild[61418:122688819] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc  | 52 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---\\n test/yang/call/python_exception_test.clj | 60 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\\n 2 files changed, 108 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)\\n# ==============================================================================\\n# datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\\n# ==============================================================================\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-07 04:30:00 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 11:30:00 +07 (+0700)\\nStatus: OWNER-APPROVED 2026-11-07 (\\\"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track.\\\"); v2 SUPERSEDES v1 (1794920000000) per the review round — architect gpt-6-astra, engineer mob gpt-6.1-sol + gemini-3.8-flash: all three ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS, folded below. Qwen aborted (headless tool denial); kimi quota-exhausted (429).\\nReview records: collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log.\\n\\n# RULING OF RECORD v2 — the exception track: §8.10.7 enforced uniformly in all four frontends\\n\\n## The decision\\n\\nException handling is enforced uniformly across yang.clojure, yang.typescript (js),\\nyang.php, and yang.python. At every admitted guest-call boundary, an escaping source\\nexception compiles down to THE SAME continuation: the caller's supplied raise exit\\nreceiving the §8.10.7 condition\\n\\n    {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value original-thrown-value}\\n\\nand every receiving runtime implements the §8.10.7 reception rules as written. This is a\\nSEPARATE TRACK: its own folder (collab/exception-track/), its own rounds, its own\\nacceptance gate; the four frontend seats cede the exception-fix surfaces to the track and\\nreceive per-frontend results back into their records.\\n\\n## Scope bounds (v2: sharpened per astra §5)\\n\\n- \\\"Every boundary\\\" means ADMITTED GUEST-CALL boundaries. VM/custody defects and host\\n  implementation failures remain operational failures (design :3904); no broad\\n  host catch-and-relabel is authorized.\\n- A RETURNED condition-shaped map is ordinary data; a THROWN condition-shaped map is the\\n  payload of a fresh condition; shape is never evidence of an existing condition to\\n  forward (the cell-ref rule, restated — R5).\\n- \\\"Same continuation\\\" does NOT require identical internal lowering (R4's allowance).\\n\\n## The requirements (v2: amended per the review round)\\n\\n**R1 — the callee bridge (js AND php non-compliant today).** Every frontend's published\\nfunction entry, closure entry, and higher-order entry installs the exception bridge\\nBEFORE argument binding: it reads the supplied `:yang.call/raise` and delivers every\\nescaping source exception AND argument-binding failure to it as the thrown condition,\\nexactly once, with execution after the call unreachable. js entries apply `:code` without\\nreading the raise exit (prelude.cljc:1581); php's function entry (:2264) and Closure entry\\n(:4217) lack it, and php's raise with an empty handler stack RETURNS normally\\n(prelude.cljc:194-197). The bridge must also satisfy §8.10.8: complete cleanup and\\nhandler/depth/frame restoration BEFORE the transfer; a later generator resume never\\nreuses a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\\n\\n**R2 — foreign reception and retained-condition lifetime (python, php non-compliant\\ntoday).** On receiving a thrown condition: (1) a payload valid in the receiving runtime\\nkeeps its ordinary catching and identity behavior — delivered UNCHANGED (python raising\\nan already-local exception raw is compliant; python raising an OTHERWISE-FOREIGN payload\\nraw at :1034 is not); (2) js may deliver the otherwise-foreign payload raw; (3) python\\nand php represent an otherwise-foreign payload with their own generated\\n`ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original payload — catch-all\\ncatches it, typed catches match the LOCAL hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names,\\nand formatting it never implicitly invokes foreign methods. Retention rides the generated\\nexception's IDENTITY for its ENTIRE LIFETIME, outside source-writable attributes, with no\\nambient registry (design :3878, :3885). Raising the whole envelope (php :2384) or an\\notherwise-foreign raw payload (python :1034) is non-compliant.\\n\\n**R3 — protocol failures retain and forward the condition (ALL FOUR frontends; v2\\nwidened per astra §1 / sol §1).** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`,\\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding` (fully qualified) map to a documented local exception\\nform retaining the complete original condition — qualified reason AND plain evidence — so\\nan unchanged rethrow FORWARDS the original condition without a new wrapper. js discards\\nthe condition into fresh text (:1627); python builds a generic TypeError (:1046); clojure\\nretains it as catch data (:1306) but public escape reclassifies through unconditional\\n`yang.call/thrown` (:1088); php constructs a fresh Error for not-callable and raises the\\nenvelope otherwise (:2384), and its source rethrow rejects that envelope (:4085). All\\nfour are in scope, reception AND outbound rethrow.\\n\\n**R4 — clojure unifies or proves equivalence.** The intra-unit outcome-token channel\\n(clojure.cljc:1115, :1125-1133) may remain an optimization only with equivalence proof\\nacross caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order\\nre-entry (§8.10.9's bar) — a single passing example is insufficient. Public\\nprotocol-rethrow repair (R3) does not wait for this decision. Clojure's restricted catch\\nsurface (single catch, no `finally`) is a source-semantics decision this ruling does not\\nchange.\\n\\n**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Per the scope bounds above; plus: forged or\\nsource-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained\\ncondition.\\n\\n## The baseline (audit of record)\\n\\nThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\\ne9a0b430) is the baseline. Citation corrections of record from the review round: js\\nfollows its GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, prelude.cljc:1704), not the host\\nengine's; php's empty-stack return is :194-197 (envelope reception :2384); clojure's\\nactual private branches are :1115 and :1125-1133 (:1110 is the packet-length guard).\\n\\n## Acceptance gate (v2: widened per astra §4 / sol §4)\\n\\nThe track is DONE when ALL of the following hold, with real source on both sides of every\\nleg:\\n\\n1. **The directed pair matrix — 12 cross-language directions** derived from the four\\n   admitted frontends (clojure↔python and clojure↔php explicitly included), plus local\\n   regressions.\\n2. **Callable-kind coverage**: named exports AND closure/higher-order entries (php\\n   Closures concretely), supported methods/callable objects per frontend capability.\\n3. **The §8.10.11 exception scenarios**: binding failures; arbitrary foreign thrown\\n   data; catch-all and typed catches (where the source has them — js has no typed-catch\\n   syntax, clojure's admitted surface rejects `finally`; exercise cleanup in a frontend\\n   that supports it and PRESERVE clojure's rejection tests); unchanged rethrow; finally\\n   replacement; protocol-error rethrow; condition-shaped thrown maps staying data;\\n   A→B→A propagation asserting original identity and cleanup order; the stored\\n   ForeignException rethrown from another activation.\\n4. **Context lifetime**: repeated success→raise→success, cross-runtime recursion,\\n   suspension/resume — handler/depth/frame state restored, exactly-once\\n   binding/cleanup.\\n5. **Host-by-VM results**: JVM, Node, and Dart across the admitted VM paths, with\\n   explicit refusals; portable parser-produced fixtures where the JVM-only kit cannot\\n   reach; bundled-walker paths where admitted, never requiring unsupported linked\\n   execution; bundled/linked parity and task isolation preserving existing custody\\n   refusals.\\n6. **Identity assertions BEFORE rendering**: original reasons, complete evidence, payload\\n   identity, wrapper non-stacking, cleanup order — equal rendered text is not identity\\n   evidence.\\n\\n## Sequencing (v2: corrected per astra §3 / sol §3 — the v1 order is REJECTED)\\n\\n0. Contract + focused FAILING fixtures first (retained-condition provenance; boundary\\n   fixtures construct malformed invocations source cannot naturally build).\\n1. **JS R1 + R3** together (R3 alone cannot cross the boundary while entries ignore the\\n   exit).\\n2. **PHP R1 + R2 + R3 as ONE coherent round** — function entry, Closure entry, and all\\n   three call helpers (php/call, call-with-binding-profile, call-named). Landing the\\n   bridge before reception repair would route local Throwables into the envelope-raising\\n   receiver and break existing local typed catches.\\n3. **Python R2 + R3 with the egress change** (prelude.cljc:912's always-fresh condition\\n   must become condition-preserving for retained wrappers).\\n4. **Clojure R3, then R4** (the optimization decision last).\\nJS and Python work proceed independently once the contract lands; no Clojure-containing\\npair declares conformance before its R3 gap closes.\\n\\n— filed by the yang.python orchestrator seat; owner decision 2026-11-07\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && python3 -B /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target 4cfa609d..a396ae28 --repo-dir /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\n\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\n\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\n\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\n\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\n\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\n\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\n\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\n\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\n2. **Explain the \\\"Why\\\"**: Don't just say \\\"change this\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\").\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-eCbBMQcV' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:12.408 xcodebuild[61340:122688135]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:12.873 xcodebuild[61340:122688133] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-juFscwzu' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 20:10:13.933 xcodebuild[61349:122688241]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 20:10:14.160 xcodebuild[61349:122688238] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n# Code Review Summary for `4cfa609d..a396ae28`\\n\\n## Executive Overview\\n- **Total Files Changed:** 2\\n- **Lines Added:** +108 | **Lines Deleted:** -4\\n- **Test-to-Source Additions Ratio:** 1.25 (60 test lines vs 48 source lines)\\n- **Overall Change Risk:** `MEDIUM`\\n\\n### Included Commits\\n- a396ae28 python egress: forward a retained generated exception's original condition\\n- 24f2f467 python R3 reception: retain the complete protocol condition\\n- d9a73150 python R2 reception: wrap foreign payloads in a generated ForeignException\\n\\n## Automated Scan Findings\\n✅ No critical security or code quality smells detected by automated scan.\\n\\n## File Churn & Risk Breakdown\\n| File Path | Category | +Add | -Del | Risk |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc` | Source Code | +48 | -4 | `Medium` |\\n| `test/yang/call/python_exception_test.clj` | Tests | +60 | -0 | `Low` |\\n\\n## Reviewer Focus & Recommendations\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*prelude*' -g '*python*' -g '*py*r2*' collab test src && command -v -a git python3 clojure bb && ls /opt/homebrew/bin/git /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/git /usr/local/bin/python3\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*prelude*' -g '*python*' -g '*py*r2*' collab test src && command -v -a git python3 clojure bb && ls /opt/homebrew/bin/git /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/git /usr/local/bin/python3\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-07 04:30:00 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 11:30:00 +07 (+0700)\\nStatus: OWNER-APPROVED 2026-11-07 (\\\"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track.\\\"); v2 SUPERSEDES v1 (1794920000000) per the review round — architect gpt-6-astra, engineer mob gpt-6.1-sol + gemini-3.8-flash: all three ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS, folded below. Qwen aborted (headless tool denial); kimi quota-exhausted (429).\\nReview records: collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log.\\n\\n# RULING OF RECORD v2 — the exception track: §8.10.7 enforced uniformly in all four frontends\\n\\n## The decision\\n\\nException handling is enforced uniformly across yang.clojure, yang.typescript (js),\\nyang.php, and yang.python. At every admitted guest-call boundary, an escaping source\\nexception compiles down to THE SAME continuation: the caller's supplied raise exit\\nreceiving the §8.10.7 condition\\n\\n    {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value original-thrown-value}\\n\\nand every receiving runtime implements the §8.10.7 reception rules as written. This is a\\nSEPARATE TRACK: its own folder (collab/exception-track/), its own rounds, its own\\nacceptance gate; the four frontend seats cede the exception-fix surfaces to the track and\\nreceive per-frontend results back into their records.\\n\\n## Scope bounds (v2: sharpened per astra §5)\\n\\n- \\\"Every boundary\\\" means ADMITTED GUEST-CALL boundaries. VM/custody defects and host\\n  implementation failures remain operational failures (design :3904); no broad\\n  host catch-and-relabel is authorized.\\n- A RETURNED condition-shaped map is ordinary data; a THROWN condition-shaped map is the\\n  payload of a fresh condition; shape is never evidence of an existing condition to\\n  forward (the cell-ref rule, restated — R5).\\n- \\\"Same continuation\\\" does NOT require identical internal lowering (R4's allowance).\\n\\n## The requirements (v2: amended per the review round)\\n\\n**R1 — the callee bridge (js AND php non-compliant today).** Every frontend's published\\nfunction entry, closure entry, and higher-order entry installs the exception bridge\\nBEFORE argument binding: it reads the supplied `:yang.call/raise` and delivers every\\nescaping source exception AND argument-binding failure to it as the thrown condition,\\nexactly once, with execution after the call unreachable. js entries apply `:code` without\\nreading the raise exit (prelude.cljc:1581); php's function entry (:2264) and Closure entry\\n(:4217) lack it, and php's raise with an empty handler stack RETURNS normally\\n(prelude.cljc:194-197). The bridge must also satisfy §8.10.8: complete cleanup and\\nhandler/depth/frame restoration BEFORE the transfer; a later generator resume never\\nreuses a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\\n\\n**R2 — foreign reception and retained-condition lifetime (python, php non-compliant\\ntoday).** On receiving a thrown condition: (1) a payload valid in the receiving runtime\\nkeeps its ordinary catching and identity behavior — delivered UNCHANGED (python raising\\nan already-local exception raw is compliant; python raising an OTHERWISE-FOREIGN payload\\nraw at :1034 is not); (2) js may deliver the otherwise-foreign payload raw; (3) python\\nand php represent an otherwise-foreign payload with their own generated\\n`ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original payload — catch-all\\ncatches it, typed catches match the LOCAL hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names,\\nand formatting it never implicitly invokes foreign methods. Retention rides the generated\\nexception's IDENTITY for its ENTIRE LIFETIME, outside source-writable attributes, with no\\nambient registry (design :3878, :3885). Raising the whole envelope (php :2384) or an\\notherwise-foreign raw payload (python :1034) is non-compliant.\\n\\n**R3 — protocol failures retain and forward the condition (ALL FOUR frontends; v2\\nwidened per astra §1 / sol §1).** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`,\\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding` (fully qualified) map to a documented local exception\\nform retaining the complete original condition — qualified reason AND plain evidence — so\\nan unchanged rethrow FORWARDS the original condition without a new wrapper. js discards\\nthe condition into fresh text (:1627); python builds a generic TypeError (:1046); clojure\\nretains it as catch data (:1306) but public escape reclassifies through unconditional\\n`yang.call/thrown` (:1088); php constructs a fresh Error for not-callable and raises the\\nenvelope otherwise (:2384), and its source rethrow rejects that envelope (:4085). All\\nfour are in scope, reception AND outbound rethrow.\\n\\n**R4 — clojure unifies or proves equivalence.** The intra-unit outcome-token channel\\n(clojure.cljc:1115, :1125-1133) may remain an optimization only with equivalence proof\\nacross caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order\\nre-entry (§8.10.9's bar) — a single passing example is insufficient. Public\\nprotocol-rethrow repair (R3) does not wait for this decision. Clojure's restricted catch\\nsurface (single catch, no `finally`) is a source-semantics decision this ruling does not\\nchange.\\n\\n**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Per the scope bounds above; plus: forged or\\nsource-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained\\ncondition.\\n\\n## The baseline (audit of record)\\n\\nThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\\ne9a0b430) is the baseline. Citation corrections of record from the review round: js\\nfollows its GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, prelude.cljc:1704), not the host\\nengine's; php's empty-stack return is :194-197 (envelope reception :2384); clojure's\\nactual private branches are :1115 and :1125-1133 (:1110 is the packet-length guard).\\n\\n## Acceptance gate (v2: widened per astra §4 / sol §4)\\n\\nThe track is DONE when ALL of the following hold, with real source on both sides of every\\nleg:\\n\\n1. **The directed pair matrix — 12 cross-language directions** derived from the four\\n   admitted frontends (clojure↔python and clojure↔php explicitly included), plus local\\n   regressions.\\n2. **Callable-kind coverage**: named exports AND closure/higher-order entries (php\\n   Closures concretely), supported methods/callable objects per frontend capability.\\n3. **The §8.10.11 exception scenarios**: binding failures; arbitrary foreign thrown\\n   data; catch-all and typed catches (where the source has them — js has no typed-catch\\n   syntax, clojure's admitted surface rejects `finally`; exercise cleanup in a frontend\\n   that supports it and PRESERVE clojure's rejection tests); unchanged rethrow; finally\\n   replacement; protocol-error rethrow; condition-shaped thrown maps staying data;\\n   A→B→A propagation asserting original identity and cleanup order; the stored\\n   ForeignException rethrown from another activation.\\n4. **Context lifetime**: repeated success→raise→success, cross-runtime recursion,\\n   suspension/resume — handler/depth/frame state restored, exactly-once\\n   binding/cleanup.\\n5. **Host-by-VM results**: JVM, Node, and Dart across the admitted VM paths, with\\n   explicit refusals; portable parser-produced fixtures where the JVM-only kit cannot\\n   reach; bundled-walker paths where admitted, never requiring unsupported linked\\n   execution; bundled/linked parity and task isolation preserving existing custody\\n   refusals.\\n6. **Identity assertions BEFORE rendering**: original reasons, complete evidence, payload\\n   identity, wrapper non-stacking, cleanup order — equal rendered text is not identity\\n   evidence.\\n\\n## Sequencing (v2: corrected per astra §3 / sol §3 — the v1 order is REJECTED)\\n\\n0. Contract + focused FAILING fixtures first (retained-condition provenance; boundary\\n   fixtures construct malformed invocations source cannot naturally build).\\n1. **JS R1 + R3** together (R3 alone cannot cross the boundary while entries ignore the\\n   exit).\\n2. **PHP R1 + R2 + R3 as ONE coherent round** — function entry, Closure entry, and all\\n   three call helpers (php/call, call-with-binding-profile, call-named). Landing the\\n   bridge before reception repair would route local Throwables into the envelope-raising\\n   receiver and break existing local typed catches.\\n3. **Python R2 + R3 with the egress change** (prelude.cljc:912's always-fresh condition\\n   must become condition-preserving for retained wrappers).\\n4. **Clojure R3, then R4** (the optimization decision last).\\nJS and Python work proceed independently once the contract lands; no Clojure-containing\\npair declares conformance before its R3 gap closes.\\n\\n— filed by the yang.python orchestrator seat; owner decision 2026-11-07\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794810000000-orchestrator-reply-yangpython-restore-landed.md\\ntest/yang/call/python_exception_test.clj\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/prelude_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/class_prelude_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_test.cljc\\ncollab/yang-python-linked-prelude/yang-python-linked-prelude-2026-10.MANIFEST.txt\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/repl/python_antlr_test.cljc\\ncollab/exception-track/1795300000000-yangclojure-ack-tsphp-ordering-and-prelude-ownership.md\\ncollab/exception-track/1794375661926-python-r2-kit-overlay-findings.md\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.prelude-parity-test.quick.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-conv-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-literal-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-ops-test.quick.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.safepoint-test.quick.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.safepoint-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.e2e-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.e2e-test.quick.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.e2e-c2-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-ops-parser-test.quick.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-ops-parser-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.prelude-parity-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.int-ops-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yin.repl.python-antlr-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yang.python.antlr.e2e-c1-test.slow.log\\ncollab/general/tiers-runs/yin.repl.python-antlr-test.quick.log\\nc