\\\t(defn- declared-functions\\\\n  3624\\\\t  \\\\\\\"Every function the unit declares: lower-cased fully qualified name to the\\\\n  3625\\\\t   vector of its by-reference parameter positions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  3626\\\\t  [ctx]\\\\n  3627\\\\t  (letfn [(refs\\\\n  3628\\\\t            [n]\\\\n  3629\\\\t            (let [fps (core/rules ctx (first (core/rules ctx n \\\\\\\"formalParameterList\\\\\\\"))\\\\n  3630\\\\t                                  \\\\\\\"formalParameter\\\\\\\")]\\\\n  3631\\\\t              (vec (keep-indexed (fn [i fp] (when (some #(p/token? % \\\\\\\"&\\\\\\\") (core/kids ctx fp)) i))\\\\n  3632\\\\t                                 fps))))\\\\n  3633\\\\t          (walk\\\\n  3634\\\\t            [ictx n]\\\\n  3635\\\\t            (into (if (p/rule? n \\\\\\\"functionDeclaration\\\\\\\")\\\\n  3636\\\\t                    {(str/lower-case (ns-prefixed ictx (function-written ctx n))) (refs n)}\\\\n  3637\\\\t                    {})\\\\n  3638\\\\t                  (mapcat #(walk ictx %))\\\\n  3639\\\\t                  (core/rule-kids ctx n)))]\\\\n  3640\\\\t    (into {} (mapcat #(walk (item-ctx ctx %) %)) (:items ctx))))\\\\n  3641\\\\t\\\\n  3642\\\\t\\\\n  3643\\\\t(def entry-path\\\\n  3644\\\\t  \\\\\\\"The entry unit's declared path when the composition names none.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  3645\\\\t  \\\\\\\"main.php\\\\\\\")\\\\n  3646\\\\t\\\\n  3647\\\\t\\\\n  3648\\\\t(defn- context\\\\n  3649\\\\t  ([packet] (context packet {}))\\\\n  3650\\\\t  ([packet {:keys [path linked?], :or {path entry-path}}]\\\\n  3651\\\\t   (let [pk (p/validate! packet {:diagnostic-ns diagnostic-ns})\\\\n  3652\\\\t         ctx {:pk pk,\\\\n  3653\\\\t              :analysis (scope/analyze pk),\\\\n  3654\\\\t              :loops [],\\\\n  3655\\\\t              :unit {:path path},\\\\n  3656\\\\t              :linked? (boolean linked?),\\\\n  3657\\\\t              :warnings (atom [])}\\\\n  3658\\\\t         ctx (merge ctx (unit-layout ctx))]\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:56:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_28\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"**ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS.** The central decision matches §8.10.7, but the ruling needs corrections to its scope, coverage, sequencing, and completion gate.\\\\n\\\\nReviewed the ruling and the audit’s last agent message against `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. The ruling and audit files were read from the main workspace because they are absent from f12. No files edited; no tests run. Behavioral findings below are static deductions.\\\\n\\\\n1. **[P1] Explicitly assign R3 to all four frontends.**\\\\n\\\\n   The audit’s third divergence includes Clojure and PHP, but the ruling’s baseline summary and sequencing reduce it to JS/Python.\\\\n\\\\n   Clojure receives a protocol failure as whole-condition catch data at [clojure.cljc:1306](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1306), then public escape unconditionally wraps that data in `:yang.call/thrown` at [1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088). Retention alone therefore does not preserve the original reason.\\\\n\\\\n   PHP constructs a fresh `Error` for `not-callable` and raises other envelopes directly at [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384). The same problem exists in `call-with-binding-profile` and `call-named`. R2’s treatment of *thrown* conditions does not automatically fix those protocol failures.\\\\n\\\\n   **Amendment:** explicitly include PHP and Clojure reception **and outbound unchanged rethrow** under R3. Use the fully qualified keywords `:yang.call/invalid-invocation` and `:yang.call/unsupported-binding`; the unqualified spellings currently printed in R3 are different keywords.\\\\n\\\\n2. **[P1] Make retained-condition forwarding and activation lifetime explicit requirements.**\\\\n\\\\n   R2 summarizes reception rules (1)–(3), but those are only part of §8.10.7. The remaining requirements include:\\\\n\\\\n   - Unchanged rethrow forwards the complete original condition without another wrapper.\\\\n   - A fresh exception creates a new condition.\\\\n   - Returning to the originating runtime restores original type and identity.\\\\n   - Retention belongs to the generated exception’s identity for its entire lifetime, outside source-writable attributes and without an ambient registry.\\\\n   - Formatting foreign payloads must not invoke foreign methods implicitly.\\\\n\\\\n   These are explicit [design requirements](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3878), not optional implementation details. The saved-wrapper acceptance case covers part of this, but does not by itself test attribute tampering, reclamation/reachability, or formatting.\\\\n\\\\n   Also incorporate [§8.10.8](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3911): establish the bridge before binding; complete cleanup and restore handler/depth/frame state before transferring; restore state on normal return; preserve exactly-once behavior across suspension. Later generator resumes must not reuse a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\\\\n\\\\n   This matters even for Python’s existing bridge: [prelude.cljc:912](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:912) always constructs a new thrown condition. Adding retained wrappers at reception without changing egress will still nest wrappers or lose protocol reasons.\\\\n\\\\n3. **[P1] Correct the sequencing; PHP bridge and reception changes must land together.**\\\\n\\\\n   The proposed order omits **R1 JS entirely**. R3 JS cannot establish cross-boundary rethrow while JS entries still ignore the supplied raise exit.\\\\n\\\\n   More immediately, **R1 PHP before R2 PHP risks breaking existing PHP-to-PHP catches**. After the bridge is installed, an escaping local Throwable will reach `php/call` inside a condition envelope. Today that helper raises the envelope itself. Local typed catches then receive a map instead of the original Throwable; explicit rethrow rejects non-Throwable values at [prelude.cljc:4085](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4085).\\\\n\\\\n   A workable order is:\\\\n\\\\n   - Specify retained-condition provenance and add focused failing fixtures.\\\\n   - Land JS R1 with R3 reception/egress.\\\\n   - Land PHP R1 with R2 and R3 reception/egress as one coherent round, including Closure entries and all three call helpers.\\\\n   - Land Python R2/R3 with the corresponding egress change.\\\\n   - Complete Clojure R3/R4, including any retained fast-path proof.\\\\n\\\\n   JS and Python work can proceed independently once the contract is fixed. Clojure need not precede the others, but Clojure-containing pairs cannot be declared conformant before its protocol-rethrow gap closes. “R3 first” is safe as preparatory work; it is insufficient as a completed semantic fix.\\\\n\\\\n4. **[P1] The stated acceptance gate is necessary but insufficient.**\\\\n\\\\n   The shared kit is a JVM `.clj` harness running linked programs on **semantic, stack, and register** VMs. It explicitly excludes the AST walker’s unsupported linked path. See [conformance.clj:23](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:23) and [run-program](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:277).\\\\n\\\\n   Amend the completion gate to require:\\\\n\\\\n   - **An explicit directed pair matrix.** Four admitted frontends imply twelve cross-language directions, including Clojure↔PHP and Clojure↔Python, plus local regressions. Derive this from admission data rather than whichever tests happen to exist.\\\\n   - **Callable-kind coverage.** Named functions alone do not prove Closure/higher-order entries, supported methods, callable objects, or builtins comply.\\\\n   - **Context-lifetime cases:** success→raise→success, re-entry, suspension/resume, and exactly-once binding/cleanup.\\\\n   - **Host-by-VM results:** JVM, Node, and Dart across all admitted VM paths, with explicit refusals. Use portable parser-produced fixtures where necessary. Test bundled AST-walker paths where admitted; do not require unsupported linked execution.\\\\n   - **Bundled/linked parity and task isolation**, preserving existing custody refusals.\\\\n   - **Guest identity assertions before rendering.** Equal rendered messages are not identity evidence.\\\\n\\\\n   These follow the adjacent [context, publication, and portability acceptance rows](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:4064). Adding exception tests only to the current kit would leave Node, Dart, and admitted fourth-VM execution unproved.\\\\n\\\\n5. **[P2] Resolve wording that otherwise overreaches or contradicts the design.**\\\\n\\\\n   - “Every escaping source exception … receiving `:yang.call/thrown`” needs an exception for **unchanged rethrows of retained protocol failures**. Those must preserve their original qualified reason. State the displayed envelope rule for **fresh source exceptions**.\\\\n   - Scope “through any boundary” to admitted guest-call boundaries. VM/custody defects and host implementation failures remain operational failures; no broad host catch-and-relabel is authorized. [Design:3904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3904)\\\\n   - R2’s “raw payload … is non-compliant” must say **otherwise foreign payload**. Delivering an already-valid local exception raw is required by reception rule (1).\\\\n   - “Every admitted pair passes those legs” needs applicability rules. JS has no typed-catch syntax; Clojure’s admitted surface rejects `finally`. Exercise cleanup in the participating frontend that supports it and preserve Clojure’s rejection tests.\\\\n   - R5 should distinguish a **returned** condition-shaped map, which is normal data, from a **thrown** condition-shaped map, which is the payload of a fresh condition.\\\\n   - R4 is consistent with the design’s permission for proven fast paths. Do not interpret “same continuation” as requiring identical internal lowering. Conversely, one exception test does not prove the full evaluation/binding/effects/suspension/identity equivalence required by §8.10.9.\\\\n\\\\n6. **[P2] Correct the citation-backed descriptions; the underlying locations mostly check out.**\\\\n\\\\n   | Checked locations | Result |\\\\n   |---|---|\\\\n   | JS [1581](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581), [1627](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1627) | Confirmed: entry directly applies private code without reading raise; reception extracts thrown payloads and discards protocol conditions when constructing TypeErrors. |\\\\n   | PHP [190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:190), [2264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2264), [2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384), [4217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4217) | Confirmed: empty-stack raise returns normally; named placement/body lack a bridge; reception raises envelopes; Closure entry drops the supplied exit and delegates through `php/call`. |\\\\n   | Python [886–912](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:886), [1034](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1034), [1046](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1046) | Confirmed: binding and body are protected; thrown reception raises raw payloads; protocol reception constructs a generic TypeError. |\\\\n   | Clojure [904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904), [1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088), [1110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1110), [1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1486) | Confirmed: error outcomes, public wrapping, authenticated private entry lane, and intra-unit fast routing. |\\\\n\\\\n   The substantive citation error is the ruling’s description of JS as following the **“host engine’s own handler stack.”** It follows the **guest JS runtime’s continuation-backed handler stack** in `js.rt/ctx`; see [js/throw and js/try](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704). The audit correctly makes this distinction.\\\\n\\\\n   Two anchors should also be tightened: PHP `2384` demonstrates envelope reception, while `190` demonstrates normal return on an empty handler stack. Clojure `1110` is the packet-length guard; `1115` and `1125–1133` show the actual private branches. These are imprecise anchors, not evidence that the private-channel claim is false.\\\\n\\\\nAll five requirements can be pinned, with the following focused evidence:\\\\n\\\\n| Requirement | Focused test |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| **R1** | Invoke a real lowered entry with a recording, non-returning raise exit. Trigger a body exception and binding/default failure; assert exact payload identity, one transfer, and no normal return. Repeat for Closure/higher-order entries. |\\\\n| **R2** | Foreign payload → local catch-all/typed catch → unchanged rethrow → origin. Assert local hierarchy, original identity, saved-wrapper lifetime, and replacement behavior. |\\\\n| **R3** | Exercise all three qualified reasons in each frontend; catch and rethrow the generated exception across another activation. Assert the entire condition, including evidence, survives. Use boundary fixtures for malformed invocations that valid source cannot naturally construct. |\\\\n| **R4** | Compare public and authenticated private paths for caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order re-entry. Preserve source-surface rejection tests; supply broader equivalence evidence if retaining the optimization. |\\\\n| **R5** | Return and throw exact condition lookalikes, including runtime-built maps; forge wrapper-looking attributes. Assert ordinary return or a fresh thrown payload, never unauthorized condition forwarding. |\\\\n\\\\nThe audit’s divergence list is substantially represented, but **Clojure/PHP protocol rethrow, JS bridge scheduling, and the broader completion evidence need explicit ownership before this is reliable as a ruling of record.**\\\"}}\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\\n\\\"free laptop\\\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\\n**Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n/Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure/1.12.4/_remote.repositories\\n/Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure/1.12.4/clojure-1.12.4.jar.sha1\\n/Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure/1.12.4/clojure-1.12.4.pom\\n/Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure/1.12.4/clojure-1.12.4.jar\\n/Users